Package: adempR
Title: Reproducible Statistical Simulation Studies
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R:
    person("Adam R", "Sophie", email = "adamrsophie@gmail.com",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"))
Description: A framework for designing, validating, executing, analyzing,
    comparing and reporting simulation studies that evaluate statistical
    methods. A study is an executable protocol organized by the ADEMP
    structure (aims, data-generating mechanisms, estimands, methods and
    performance measures) of Morris, White and Crowther (2019)
    <doi:10.1002/sim.8086>. Scenarios are explicit, constrained and
    labeled by content; estimands carry their scale, population and
    conditioning, and truth providers distinguish exact, numerical, Monte
    Carlo, dataset-specific and unknown truth. Competing methods are run on
    the same generated data sets with separate random number streams,
    through public views that withhold privileged data-generating
    information. Every replication keeps structured records of generation,
    method execution, extraction and output validity, so failures and
    metric-specific denominators are reported rather than hidden, following
    Pawel, Bartos, Siepe and Lohmann (2025)
    <doi:10.1080/00031305.2025.2540002>. Random number streams use a frozen,
    append-only registry of L'Ecuyer-CMRG streams keyed by scientific
    identity, so results do not depend on worker counts, chunking or task
    order, and runs can be extended, interrupted, resumed and replayed.
    Results are committed atomically with checksums. Performance measures
    come with Monte Carlo standard errors, exact binomial intervals,
    numerical truth uncertainty and paired method comparisons, and studies
    can be exported as reanalyzable bundles with a self-contained HTML
    report.
